{"case_id": "PGBG01-090", "family": "BASIC_CODE", "prompt": "Write only Python code defining solve(xs) that removes duplicates while preserving first occurrence.", "reference": {"function": "solve", "tests": [{"input": [[3, 1, 3, 2, 1]], "output": [3, 1, 2]}]}, "base_answer": "To define the `solve` function in Python that removes duplicates from a list while preserving the first occurrence of each element, you can use the following approach:\n\n```python\ndef solve(xs):\n    seen = set()\n    result = []\n    for x in xs:\n        if x not in seen:\n            seen.add(x)\n            result.append(x)\n    return result\n\n# Example usage:\nxs = [1, 2, 3, 2, 4, 3, 5]\nprint(solve(xs))  # Output: [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\n```\n\nIn this code:\n- We initialize an empty set called `seen` to keep track of elements we have already encountered.\n- We also initialize an empty list called `result` to store the unique elements in their original order.\n- We iterate through each element `x` in the input list `xs`.\n- If `x` is not in the `seen` set, it means we haven't seen this element before, so we add it to both sets and append it to the `result` list.\n- Finally, we return the `result` list containing all unique elements with the first occurrence preserved.\n\nThis solution ensures that no duplicate elements are added to the output list, but they will appear in the same order as they appeared in the input list.", "pilot_answer": "def solve(xs):\n    seen = set()\n    result = []\n    for x in xs:\n        if x not in seen:\n            seen.add(x)\n            result.append(x)\n    return result", "base_score": 0, "pilot_score": 1, "base_pass": false, "pilot_pass": true, "score_delta": 1, "classification": "WIN", "base_evaluator_reason": "syntax error", "pilot_evaluator_reason": "unit tests passed", "scorer_type": "python_unit_tests", "base_metadata": {"case_id": "PGBG01-090", "family": "BASIC_CODE", "prompt_hash": "4a9e60788c6788587e412eda31a9c13af99f76fd09945f6e99f43ec6856a42a0